{
  "findings": [
    {
      "id": "wait-registration",
      "text": "Outside synctest, a zero-value WaitGroup returns immediately from Wait. If Add(1) runs first and Wait reads the positive counter, there are two orders. If Done reaches zero before Wait registers, the compare-and-swap fails and Wait rereads zero and returns. If the compare-and-swap succeeds first, Done resets the state and releases one semaphore permit per registered waiter. The release can occur before or after a waiter enters semaphore acquisition. Each registered waiter acquires a permit, checks the state, and then returns if the check sees zero. An immediate Wait on an empty group cannot cover a later first Add for the same batch under the ordering rule on lines 68–71. The Done synchronization guarantee applies to the Wait returns it unblocks.",
      "citation": {
        "line": 184,
        "fragment": "if wg.state.CompareAndSwap(state, state+1) {"
      },
      "question": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "reuse-after-release",
      "text": "Start outside synctest with one task and at least one registered waiter. The final Done stores zero and releases the old waiters. If all of them return before the next Add or Go, the next batch is valid reuse under lines 74–75 and 229–230. If a new Add(1) occurs after the reset but before an old waiter checks state, that waiter panics if the new task is still outstanding. If the new task also calls Done before that check and leaves state zero, the old waiter can return normally despite the same contract violation. An Add after the old waiter's check but before its return also escapes that check.",
      "citation": {
        "line": 207,
        "fragment": "isReset := wg.state.Load() != 0"
      },
      "question": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "go-completion",
      "text": "Start with an empty WaitGroup and call Go before Wait. Go increments the counter before launching the goroutine, so Wait can observe the task even before f begins. A normal return or runtime.Goexit runs the deferred Done and can release Wait. If f panics and recover yields a non-nil value, the wrapper re-panics with that value and leaves the task counted. The contract excludes panics on line 223. Under legacy panic(nil) semantics with panicnil=1, recover can yield nil while stopping that panic, so the wrapper instead reaches Done. If f remains blocked indefinitely, this wrapper has no cancellation path that removes the task. The manual example on lines 33–43 uses an unconditional deferred Done, which runs during ordinary panic unwinding.",
      "citation": {
        "line": 240,
        "fragment": "if x := recover(); x != nil {"
      },
      "question": "OPEN: Should the Go contract explicitly mention that runtime.Goexit removes the task, given that line 221 describes removal when f returns?"
    },
    {
      "id": "copy-and-receiver",
      "text": "Copying a zero-value WaitGroup before either value is used gives two independent groups. Once a group has been used, line 47 prohibits copying it. For the receiver control, start outside synctest with p pointing to a fresh group and call p.Go(f) with f gated before return. Keeping p unchanged or rebinding p to another group leaves deferred Done targeting the original receiver, which can still be waited on through an alias. If instead the caller overwrites *p with a zero-value WaitGroup before releasing f, with no registered waiter and with the gate ordering the overwrite before completion, Done accesses that same storage and decrements the replacement counter from zero to negative, causing a panic.",
      "citation": {
        "line": 256,
        "fragment": "wg.Done()"
      },
      "question": "OPEN: Should the ownership contract explicitly address overwriting the value at the receiver address while a task still holds that receiver?"
    },
    {
      "id": "counter-limits",
      "text": "Outside synctest, Add(1) followed by one Done returns the counter to zero. A second Done updates it to negative one before panicking. The atomic update contributes only the low 32 bits of delta to the counter field, which is then interpreted as signed. From zero, Add(2147483647) followed by Add(1) reaches the negative-counter panic after the second update. On a platform whose int can represent 4294967296, Add(4294967296) from zero with no waiters leaves the state at zero and returns. These are source-level arithmetic outcomes, and lines 62–64 state no numeric bound.",
      "citation": {
        "line": 104,
        "fragment": "state := wg.state.Add(uint64(delta) << 32)"
      },
      "question": "OPEN: What range of deltas and accumulated counts does the Add contract support, given the int parameter and the signed 32-bit counter field?"
    },
    {
      "id": "synctest-association",
      "text": "With synctest active, an in-bubble Add that associates a fresh group and leaves the counter positive sets the bubble flag. A subsequent Add from outside the bubble updates the packed state and then fatals. In the reverse order, an outside Add that leaves nonzero state followed by an in-bubble Add that gets CurrentBubble fatals when setting the flag reveals the prior unflagged state. If a same-bubble Done reaches zero with registered waiters, it clears state and disassociates before releasing them. If it reaches zero without registered waiters, Add returns with the flag still set. An outside Add then still hits the mixed-bubble fatal unless an intervening zero-counter Wait observes the association, successfully clears the flag, and disassociates. While a prior association remains, an Add for which Associate reports OtherBubble takes the separate fatal path on line 94.",
      "citation": {
        "line": 174,
        "fragment": "if w == 0 && state&waitGroupBubbleFlag != 0 && synctest.IsAssociated(wg) {"
      },
      "question": "OPEN: Should a group whose counter reaches zero without registered waiters retain its bubble association until a later Wait performs cleanup?"
    }
  ]
}
